body:
Could we also compare a method against an inherited property or non-callable attribute? Neither override check reports this:

```python
class Base:
    @property
    def value(self) -> int:
        return 1

class Child(Base):
    def value(self) -> int:
        return 1
```

A read through `Base` expects an `int` but receives a bound method. The method checker also gives up because the inherited property result is not callable. The reverse replacement, an attribute or property overriding a method, needs a comparison too.

In general, I'm not sure if it makes sense to separate method override checks from attribute override checks in the implementation as much as we do currently (though we don't necessarily need to rework that right now). All members (method or attribute) always need to be checked against whatever is on the parent of the same name (method or attribute), and in principle the core nature of the check should always be the same: is the subclass type assignable to the superclass type.

