body:
As I understand it, `сlippy` served as the reference for this? `clippy` also has priorities for categories, and as I understand it, if we add a `pep8` category in the future, it will conflict with `formatting`, since they share the same rules that cannot work together because doing so would defeat the purpose.

So does that mean we’ll need to impl prio as well?

```toml
[lints.clippy]
perf       = { level = "deny", priority = 1 }
style      = { level = "deny", priority = -1 }
pedantic   = { level = "deny", priority = -1 }
nursery    = { level = "deny", priority = -1 }
complexity = { level = "deny", priority = -1 }
suspicious = { level = "deny", priority = -1 }
```

The latest ecosystem report also shows a separate Prefect regression at `src/prefect/tasks.py:1227`: the `run_task(...)` call changes from an existing `invalid-return-type` diagnostic to a new `no-matching-overload`. It looks like this is something else that should be fixed by #26680; it results from return-context mappings being injected as preferred mappings and wrongly influencing overload selection. Might at least be worth adding such a case as a test with TODO, pending fix in #26680. Minimized reproducer:

```py
from __future__ import annotations

from typing import Generic, Literal, Never, ParamSpec, TypeVar, overload

P = ParamSpec("P")
T = TypeVar("T")
R = TypeVar("R")


class State(Generic[T]):
    pass


class Task(Generic[P, T]):
    def __call__(
        self: Task[..., T] | Task[..., Never],
        return_state: bool = False,
    ) -> T | State[T] | None:
        return_type = "state" if return_state else "result"
        return run_task(task=self, return_type=return_type)


@overload
def run_task(
    task: Task[P, R],
    return_type: Literal["state"] = "state",
) -> State[R]: ...


@overload
def run_task(
    task: Task[P, R],
    return_type: Literal["result"] = "result",
) -> R: ...


def run_task(
    task: Task[P, R],
    return_type: Literal["state", "result"] = "result",
) -> R | State[R]:
    raise NotImplementedError
```

