body:
It's quite hard to argue with these performance numbers! But I have to say, I'm still not really a huge fan of this PR. It still feels like it's adding quite a bit of confusing, ad-hoc complexity to a file that contains some of the most important -- and subtle, and (we've seen many times in the past) hard-to-maintain -- code in ty.

I wonder if one way of reducing the feeling of complexity here might be to separate the "should we cache this?" predicate into a standalone (cached?) method on `Type`? E.g.

```rs
impl Type<'db> {
    fn is_or_contains_protocol(self, db: &'db dyn Db) -> bool {
        any_over_type(db, self.resolve_type_alias(db), false, |ty| {
            match ty {
                Type::ProtocolInstance(_) => true,
                Type::SubclassOf(target) -> matches!(target.subclass_of(), SubclassOfInner::Protocol(_)),
                _ => false,
            }
        )
    }
}
```

but I already feel like I've probably taken up too much of your time discussing various experiments, so I don't want that to block the PR

I thought about this some more and came to the conclusion that we shouldn't allow this. Replacing methods through `type[Base]` is unsound:

```py
class Base:
    def method(self) -> int:
        return 0

class Child(Base):
    # valid override
    def method(self) -> bool:
        return True


def replacement(self: Base) -> int:
    return 42

def patch(cls: type[Base]) -> None:
    cls.method = replacement  # accepted by this PR.


patch(Child)

result: bool = Child().method()  # oops, contains 42, but the type is `bool`.
```
